{
  "entity0": {
    "rationale": "Across the three races, the best score (63.0, iteration 0) came from the iteration 0 geometry. Every run ended off track with max_error of 0.060 to 0.066 m and about 1.0 line-loss events, and none finished. Iterations 1 and 2 changed several things at once and scored lower, and iteration 2 also widened the sensor span to 0.04 m with 16 mm footprints. It still produced 1.0 line-loss events and max_error near 0.06 m, so sensor coverage does not look like the limit. The off-track limit is fixed at 0.06 m for every allowed body width, so body width cannot help. Mass is the one variable that moved monotonically across the races: 0.7 kg scored 63.0, 0.6 kg scored 53.7 and 0.5 kg scored 46.5. The changes were confounded, but the trend runs against the R1 suggestion to go lighter. A heavier robot should hold the tape better under the motor mismatch and slip, at the cost of a slower turn response. I therefore revert to the iteration 0 geometry in full and change only mass, from 0.7 kg to 0.9 kg. I expect fewer line losses, max_error held below the 0.06 m limit for longer, and progress above the iteration 0 values. If the result is worse, the mass trend was a confound, and the next test should move the sensor span alone.",
    "geometry": {
      "sensor_positions": [
        {"x": 0.03, "y": 0.05},
        {"x": 0.015, "y": 0.05},
        {"x": 0.0, "y": 0.05},
        {"x": -0.015, "y": 0.05},
        {"x": -0.03, "y": 0.05}
      ],
      "sensor_size": 0.012,
      "wheel_track": 0.12,
      "wheel_radius": 0.03,
      "body_width": 0.12,
      "body_length": 0.16,
      "mass": 0.9
    },
    "intent": "Test whether added mass (traction and stability) reduces the single line loss and the drift to the 0.06 m off-track limit. This is a one-variable change from iteration 0: mass goes from 0.7 kg to 0.9 kg, and sensors, footprint, wheel track, wheel radius and body are all held. Expected: line_loss_events fall below 1.0 per run, max_error stays under 0.06 m for longer, and progress rises above the 0.23 (oval), 0.14 (s-bend) and 0.10 (slalom) seen in iteration 0. The Integrator should check whether the slower turn response needs a larger kp or kd.",
    "feedback_request": {
      "questions": [
        "With mass at 0.9 kg, does the robot respond too slowly on the slalom and s-bend, so that max_error grows through the bends? If so, is more gain or derivative action needed?",
        "Does the first line loss still occur at the same point on each track? If it is at the start pose, the cause is initial alignment, not drift.",
        "Does steering_oscillation stay near the iteration 0 level (0.13 to 0.15), or does the extra inertia make the robot overshoot?",
        "Is the line-loss behaviour (line_loss setting) causing the drift off the track? Would 'last_direction' recover better than the current search setting?"
      ]
    },
    "learning": "Iterations 0, 1 and 2 all show about 1.0 line loss per run and max_error at the 0.06 m limit, whatever the sensor span, footprint, mass or track. The best result so far used the heaviest mass tried (0.7 kg), and score fell with each step down in mass. Sensor coverage has been tested at 12 and 16 mm and at 0.06 and 0.08 m span with no change in line losses, so the next lever to check is mass.
